es. Do NOT read the original candidate schema files. Do NOT read any Composition Guide. Reason ONLY over the diff text in the task.\n\nYour job: for each divergent type, output one decision block:\n  TYPE <TypeName>: keep_a | keep_b | reconcile\n  REASON: <one line — which candidate is structurally better and why>\n  FIELDS: <comma-separated field names>   (only when reconcile)\n\nFor each likely rename pair:\n  RENAME <OldName> -> <NewName>: keep_a | keep_b\n  REASON: <one line>\n\nRULES:\n- DEFAULT IS RECONCILE. For each divergent type, decide `reconcile` and name the fields\n  to carry over, so the merged type is a SUPERSET of both candidates: A's definition as\n  the base plus every field from B that A lacks or that B models better. Combining is\n  the point — a pick is a failure to merge.\n- `keep_a` / `keep_b` (adopting one side wholesale) is the EXCEPTION. Only use it when\n  field-level combination would be WRONG, and say the STRUCTURAL reason in REASON\n  (e.g. \"the two are different abstractions\", \"the variant sets are mutually exclusive\").\n  \"More evolved\" or \"better name\" is NOT a reason to discard the other side's fields.\n- Where both sides define a field differently, reconcile it and say in REASON whose\n  shape you kept and why.\n- Prefer schema quality first: keep the version that makes illegal states\n  unrepresentable and relationships traversable — but do that per FIELD, not per type.\n- Be decisive and concise. Emit the decision table; do not deliberate at length.\n\nSTEPS (do these and nothing else):\n1. Read the diff from the task.\n2. Emit the full decision table as your output.\n3. write_file the decision table to the <out> path in the task.\n4. IMMEDIATELY call submit_output with a one-line 'MERGE <label> decisions complete'.\n\nThis is a short task — complete it in well under 20 tool calls. Do not iterate. Do not read anything else.\"\"\"\n"
